      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_e4d04ef766cae51cbd6d13cfdba1e95dcad3fa468dda845ca2b0d80c2bdd6c20",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Changing a family average changes both which arithmetic terms survive and which population is being observed. Character orthogonality diagonalizes primitive-character moments; in the quadratic subfamily, prime squares survive and shift the center of log central values. Separately, a Petersson trace formula accesses a weighted Siegel family, so its low-zero statistic need not estimate the unweighted counting-family law even when representation theory fixes the underlying spin symmetry data.",
